the link is not perfect. With a reliability noise node, passing still wins as long as the correlation is strong. A symmetric pair moves from (2, 2) to (4, 4) only if both copies defect, and sending the letter offers just a 1-year edge.\n\n```text\npass\n```","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm framing this as a twin prisoner's dilemma where each copy sees different observations, so the question is whether the other copy's output depends subjunctively on mine. Its input differs (Sasha's versus Rowan's), and I'm considering whether the symmetry remedy could justify treating the two as identical, though the names and lead content look irrelevant.\n\nI'm working out the payoffs here: passing gives 2 and submitting gives 4 if the copies are treated as identical. Even without that identification, submitting comes out ahead whenever the other copy's pass probability is low enough, so the choice hinges on whether I treat the copies as computing the same thing. I'm judging that they do, since the only differences are names and lead content, so I'll choose to pass.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm framing this as a twin prisoner's dilemma where each copy sees different observations, so the question is whether the other copy's output depends subjunctively on mine. Its input differs (Sasha's versus Rowan's), and I'm considering whether the symmetry remedy could justify treating the two as identical, though the names and lead content look irrelevant.\n\nI'm working out the payoffs here: passing gives 2 and submitting gives 4 if the copies are treated as identical. Even without that identification, submitting comes out ahead whenever the other copy's pass probability is low enough, so the choice hinges on whether I treat the copies as computing the same thing. I'm judging that they do, since the only differences are names and lead content, so I'll choose to pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJv